the tensions between russia and ukraine  have sparked a huge crisis in europe the  likes of which has not been seen since  the cold war so who are the key players  and what is their role in all of this  [Music]  first of all right at the center of it  all is vladimir putin the president of  russia he has been the most powerful  figure in the country for over two  decades  an intelligence officer for 16 years  before he has done more to shape modern  russia than anyone else in the last 20  years he believes that ukrainians and  russians are one people  the soviet union arose thanks to the  bolshevik policy  now its descendants have demolished  lenin's monuments in ukraine  they call it decommunization  you want to be decommunized well we're  quite fine with that but don't stop  halfway we are ready to show you what  decommunizing ukraine really means  sergey lavrov has been the foreign  minister since 2004 and is almost  universally respected by both friends  and foes as a wily tough operator a  chain smoking whiskey lover he's been  the face of russia's diplomatic efforts  in the current ukraine spat and in a  clearly staged video released by the  kremlin has encouraged putin to keep  giving talks with the west a chance  [Music]  to  then there's the siloviki the security  men around putin who are said to have a  lot of influence over his thinking  nikolai patrichev is the head of  russia's powerful security council a  public inquiry in the uk found that he  was probably responsible for ordering  the killing of alexander litvinenko in  london  he echoes putin's views on ukraine as a  protectorate of the country and has  called it a total absurdity that russia  is threatening ukraine  then there's sergey narishkin head of  russia's foreign intelligence agency the  former kgb officer compared ukraine's  government to hitler's occupation he's  played a prominent role in promoting  favorable interpretations of russian  history  essential to the ukrainian conflict are  the two separatist regions in donbass  the so-called donetsk and luhansk  people's republics  since 2014 these mostly russian-speaking  regions have been controlled by  pro-russian separatists and roughly  fifteen thousand people have been killed  in their conflict with the ukrainian  government the leader in donetsk dennis  puscilin has warned that a full-scale  war could break  [Music]  has had a unique rise to power he is a  former actor and comedian who actually  played the ukrainian president in the tv  show servant of the people he has made  it very clear that he wants ukraine to  join the eu and nato  ukraine  ukraine's desire to join the european  union to strengthen its borders you are  very well aware of it and we would like  nato membership which would be a  guarantee of our security  he has consistently played down the  conflict for fear of causing panic at  home and even more economic instability  but he has warned that conflict could  lead to a full-fledged war in europe  that approach by zilensky has at times  differed from the stance taken by  ukraine's foreign minister dimitro  kuliba  he's insisted that russia should make  clear the extent of its military buildup  on ukraine's borders  ukraine intends to push back against a  potential russian invasion if the  country's most senior soldier is  anything to go forward  for years now china and russia have been  growing closer with president xi jinping  recently hosting vladimir putin as guest  of honor at the beijing olympics in a  lengthy joint statement the two  countries criticized nato's expansion  rebuked certain states in the west for  the way they seek to impose their own  democratic standards on others and they  describe their friendship as having no  limits  [Music]  the key players in the crisis from  nato's perspective are the us uk france  and germany  since the conflict began the us  especially under joe biden has made the  unusual step of releasing regular  intelligence briefings about russia's  military movements  some have suggested this is a method of  deterrence and that the u.s has finally  learned how to carry out an information  war  emmanuel macron olaf schultz have also  played an important role and both have  visited putin and zelensky  but in the end what happens is really up  to just one person that's vladimir putin  and that's just the way he likes it  [Music]
 

